+  <section class="annotations">
+    <h2>What Board is, and what it gives up</h2>
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Persists {column, slotIndex} per node, plus the edge list.</strong> The
+        board container (<code>#graph</code>) carries one attribute, <code>data-ui</code>,
+        holding a JSON object shaped <code>{ nodes: [{id, column, slotIndex}], edges: [[from,
+        to]] }</code>. A node's <code>column</code> is fixed by its kind — DataSource always
+        lands in lane 0, Histogram always in lane 4 — and is read back from wherever that
+        node's element actually sits in the live DOM at persist time, not carried as separate
+        mutable state that could drift from the render. <code>slotIndex</code> is that node's
+        position within its own lane's list, likewise read from DOM order. Node kind itself
+        stays on the node's own element (<code>data-node-kind</code>), the same place Beamline
+        and Bench put it, because kind is analysis content the run payload carries, not a
+        layout fact. <code>verify.py --board</code> reads this attribute after a real slot
+        move, not just at load, so the claim covers a moved node, not only a spawned one.</li>
+      <li><strong>Both connection gestures work, plus a keyboard path.</strong> An output port
+        carries a <code>pointerdown</code> listener for drag-to-connect (drop on an input port
+        to finish, the same mechanics as Bench) and a plain <code>click</code> listener for
+        click-to-connect (click an output port to arm it, then an input port to finish, the
+        same mechanics as Beamline). A native <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> already turns an
+        Enter or Space keypress into a synthesized <code>click</code>, so the same click
+        handler that answers a mouse click also answers the keyboard, with no separate keydown
+        listener needed for it. Bench refused click on purpose, and Beamline refused drag on
+        purpose — Board is the one style asked to accept both, so both are wired deliberately
+        here rather than one falling out as an accident of a shared handler.</li>
+      <li><strong>The plot lives inside the graph, as the Histogram lane's own content.</strong>
+        Every Histogram node's card carries a <code>.plot-embed</code> figure
+        (<code>data-wf="plot"</code>) sized at the reference figure's own fixed 650×460 CSS
+        px, the intrinsic size D-003's <code>plot.html</code> renders at and does not reflow
+        below. That size does not fit a 768px-wide viewport once the other four lanes sit
+        beside it, so the whole board scrolls horizontally inside its own wrapper
+        (<code>.board-wrap</code>, <code>overflow-x: auto</code>) — the same device Bench's
+        canvas and D-003's own figure already use — rather than the page body ever gaining
+        horizontal scroll.</li>
+      <li><strong>No inline <code>style=</code> attribute, anywhere.</strong> A node's lane and
+        slot are both facts about where its element sits in ordinary DOM flow — which
+        <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> it is a child of, and at what index — so placing a node needs
+        no coordinate and no <code>style=</code> at all; reordering within a lane is plain
+        <code>insertBefore</code>/<code>appendChild</code>. The one thing this style still
+        draws as geometry rather than flow is the connector between two ports, since a
+        drawn line needs endpoints to draw between: those live in the edges layer's own SVG
+        <code>&lt;path d&gt;</code> and the overlay <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code>'s own
+        <code>width</code>/<code>height</code> attributes, computed from each port's live
+        position and written as plain SVG geometry attributes, never a <code>style</code>
+        property — the same category of exception Bench's <code>&lt;foreignObject x y&gt;</code>
+        already makes for the same reason.</li>
+      <li><strong>The trade this style makes, on purpose.</strong> Board gives up Bench's free
+        spatial arrangement — a node cannot be dragged next to an unrelated node just because
+        the student wants them near each other — and gives up Beamline's single-glance
+        simplicity, since five lanes read as more machinery than one rail. What it buys instead
+        is a structure that answers a question neither of the other two can: which pipeline
+        stage is this node in, at a glance, from its column alone, independent of the order it
+        happened to be added — the same {column, slotIndex} shape the real run payload's own
+        "ui" object would need to carry, whichever style ships.</li>
+      <li><strong>Locked node kind.</strong> The palette's final tile is not one of the 8
+        addable kinds — it stands for a node type a later mission unlocks, shown so a student
+        can see it is coming. It carries no port, no click handler, and no tab stop, so it
+        cannot be reached as an active control by keyboard or by mouse.</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p>Nothing on this page ships; it exists to be compared against Beamline (D-004, single
+      rail, click-to-connect) and Bench (D-005, free canvas, drag-to-connect) at the D-007
+      checkpoint. See <code>verify.py --board</code> for what is actually measured about this
+      rendering, rather than claimed here.</p>
+  </section>
+
+</main>
+<!-- Inlined, not fetched by src=: a plain, unflagged Chromium refuses to load a file:// <script type="module" src=...> as a cross-origin fetch (CORS on the module loader specifically), so this page would be empty for any student who opens it without a server -- the exact defect D-005 cycle 1 shipped and cycle 2 fixed by inlining. The script stays type="module" (module scope, no leaked globals). This inline block is the only copy of this script -- there is no separate board.js file. -->
+<script type="module">
+/* D-006 exploration: style C, "Board".
+ *
+ * Not part of the shipping app. Demonstrates the third of the three
+ * persistence/interaction models the user asked to compare (Beamline,
+ * D-004, ordered edge list, click-to-connect; Bench, D-005, {x, y} free
+ * canvas, drag-to-connect): five TYPED COLUMNS, one per pipeline stage, each
+ * an ordinary DOM list a node lives inside. A node's position is entirely a
+ * fact about DOM structure -- which list, and at what index -- never a
+ * coordinate.
+ *
+ * Persistence lives in one attribute, `data-ui`, on `#graph`:
+ *   { "nodes": [ { "id": "n1", "column": 0, "slotIndex": 0 }, ... ],
+ *     "edges": [ ["n1", "n2"], ... ] }
+ * Both `column` and `slotIndex` are read back from the live DOM at persist
+ * time (which <ul> a node's card sits in, and at what index in that list),
+ * not tracked as separate mutable state that could drift from what is
+ * actually rendered. Node *kind* is not in it; that lives on each node's
+ * own element (`data-node-kind`), the same place Beamline and Bench put it.
+ *
+ * Connections: an output port answers both a real pointer drag (drop on an
+ * input port) and a real click (click the output port to arm it, then an
+ * input port to finish) -- the union of what Bench and Beamline each
+ * deliberately refuse one half of. A drag that moves the pointer past a
+ * small threshold is handled entirely inside the drag's own pointerup
+ * handler and marks the port so the click handler ignores the click that
+ * would otherwise still follow a down/up pair on the same element; a
+ * plain click (no meaningful movement) falls through to the click handler
+ * unchanged. A native <button> turns Enter/Space into a synthesized click,
+ * so the same click handler is the keyboard path too -- no separate keydown
+ * listener answers Enter/Space here.
+ *
+ * No inline `style=` attribute is used anywhere for node placement -- a
+ * node's lane and slot are exactly which list it is a child of and at what
+ * index, both ordinary DOM facts. The one place this file does write SVG
+ * geometry (not CSS, not `style=`) is the edges overlay: each connector's
+ * own `<path d>`, and the overlay `<svg>`'s own `width`/`height`
+ * attributes, both computed from live port positions -- the same kind of
+ * exception Bench's `<foreignObject x y>` makes, for the same reason: a
+ * drawn line needs endpoints to draw between.
+ *
+ * The 8 addable node kinds and the legal source -> destination pairs are
+ * the same facts Beamline and Bench source from the vendored reference's
+ * own allowlist (`ui/graph.py:_VALID_CONNECTIONS`) -- see beamline.js's own
+ * comment for how that was checked. Bare "Observable" stays excluded for
+ * the same reason.
+ */
+
+const SVG_NS = "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
+
+const NODE_KINDS = [
+  { kind: "DataSource", label: "Data", cls: "data", column: 0, hasIn: false, hasOut: true },
+  { kind: "Multiplicity", label: "Multiplicity", cls: "multiplicity", column: 1, hasIn: true, hasOut: true },
+  { kind: "Selection", label: "Selection", cls: "selection", column: 2, hasIn: true, hasOut: true },
+  { kind: "ObsGlobal", label: "Obs: Global", cls: "obs-global", column: 3, hasIn: true, hasOut: true },
+  { kind: "ObsObject", label: "Obs: Object", cls: "obs-object", column: 3, hasIn: true, hasOut: true },
+  { kind: "ObsVectorSum", label: "Obs: Vec Sum", cls: "obs-vecsum", column: 3, hasIn: true, hasOut: true },
+  { kind: "ObsCustom", label: "Obs: Custom", cls: "obs-custom", column: 3, hasIn: true, hasOut: true },
+  { kind: "Histogram", label: "Histogram", cls: "histogram", column: 4, hasIn: true, hasOut: false },
+];
+
+const KIND_BY_NAME = Object.fromEntries(NODE_KINDS.map((k) => [k.kind, k]));
+
+const VALID_CONNECTIONS = {
+  DataSource: ["Multiplicity", "Selection"],
+  Multiplicity: ["Multiplicity", "Selection"],
+  Selection: ["Selection", "ObsGlobal", "ObsObject", "ObsVectorSum", "ObsCustom"],
+  ObsGlobal: ["Histogram"],
+  ObsObject: ["Histogram"],
+  ObsVectorSum: ["Histogram"],
+  ObsCustom: ["Histogram"],
+  Histogram: [],
+};
+
+function isLegal(srcKind, dstKind) {
+  return (VALID_CONNECTIONS[srcKind] || []).includes(dstKind);
+}
+
+const DRAG_THRESHOLD = 5; // px of pointer movement before a press counts as a drag, not a click
+
+// The only state this file keeps beyond the DOM itself. `nodes` carries
+// kind only -- column and slotIndex are always read back from live DOM
+// structure (persistUI), never duplicated here, so there is exactly one
+// place either fact can live. `edges` is the same ordered [from, to] list
+// shape Beamline and Bench both persist.
+const graphState = {
+  nodes: new Map(), // id -> { kind }
+  edges: [], // [fromId, toId][]
+  nextId: 1,
+  keyboardArmed: null, // { id, kind } while an out-port is armed
+};
+
+let els = {};
+
+function nodeLabel(id) {
+  const n = graphState.nodes.get(id);
+  return n ? `${KIND_BY_NAME[n.kind].label} (${id})` : id;
+}
+
+function persistUI() {
+  const nodes = [];
+  for (let col = 0; col < 5; col += 1) {
+    const list = els.columnLists[col];
+    Array.from(list.children).forEach((card, slotIndex) => {
+      nodes.push({ id: card.dataset.nodeId, column: col, slotIndex });
+    });
+  }
+  els.graph.setAttribute("data-ui", JSON.stringify({ nodes, edges: graphState.edges }));
+}
+
+function setStatus(text) {
+  els.status.textContent = text;
+}
+
+function portElFor(id, role) {
+  return els.graph.querySelector(`.node-card[data-node-id="${id}"] .port--${role}`);
+}
+
+function boardRelativePoint(clientX, clientY) {
+  const boardRect = els.board.getBoundingClientRect();
+  return { x: clientX - boardRect.left, y: clientY - boardRect.top };
+}
+
+function boardRelativeCenter(el) {
+  const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
+  return boardRelativePoint(r.left + r.width / 2, r.top + r.height / 2);
+}
+
+function renderEdges() {
+  const boardRect = els.board.getBoundingClientRect();
+  const w = Math.max(els.board.scrollWidth, boardRect.width, 1);
+  const h = Math.max(els.board.scrollHeight, boardRect.height, 1);
+  els.svg.setAttribute("width", String(w));
+  els.svg.setAttribute("height", String(h));
+  els.edgesLayer.textContent = "";
+  graphState.edges.forEach(([from, to]) => {
+    const outEl = portElFor(from, "out");
+    const inEl = portElFor(to, "in");
+    if (!outEl || !inEl) return;
+    const p1 = boardRelativeCenter(outEl);
+    const p2 = boardRelativeCenter(inEl);
+    const midX = (p1.x + p2.x) / 2;
+    const path = document.createElementNS(SVG_NS, "path");
+    path.setAttribute("class", "edge");
+    path.setAttribute(
+      "d",
+      `M ${p1.x} ${p1.y} C ${midX} ${p1.y}, ${midX} ${p2.y}, ${p2.x} ${p2.y}`
+    );
+    path.setAttribute("marker-end", "url(#board-arrow)");
+    els.edgesLayer.appendChild(path);
+  });
+}
+
+function renderLinksFor(id) {
+  const card = els.graph.querySelector(`.node-card[data-node-id="${id}"]`);
+  if (!card) return;
+  const links = card.querySelector(".node-card__links");
+  links.textContent = "";
+  graphState.edges
+    .filter(([from]) => from === id)
+    .forEach(([, to]) => {
+      const li = document.createElement("li");
+      li.textContent = `→ ${nodeLabel(to)}`;
+      links.appendChild(li);
+    });
+}
+
+function buildPlotEmbed() {
+  const figure = document.createElement("figure");
+  figure.className = "plot-embed";
+  figure.dataset.wf = "plot";
+
+  const ylab = document.createElement("span");
+  ylab.className = "plot-embed__ylab";
+  ylab.textContent = "events";
+  figure.appendChild(ylab);
+
+  const area = document.createElement("div");
+  area.className = "plot-embed__area";
+  const hist = document.createElement("div");
+  hist.className = "hist";
+  const heights = ["b1", "b1", "b1", "b2", "b2", "b2", "b3", "b3", "b4", "b5", "b6", "b8", "b10", "b9", "b5", "b3", "b2", "b1", "b1", "b1"];
+  heights.forEach((cls) => {
+    const bar = document.createElement("span");
+    bar.className = cls;
+    hist.appendChild(bar);
+  });
+  area.appendChild(hist);
+  figure.appendChild(area);
+
+  const caption = document.createElement("figcaption");
+  caption.className = "plot-embed__xlab";
+  caption.textContent = "m(l1, l2) [GeV]  0 · 30 · 60 · 90 · 120";
+  figure.appendChild(caption);
+
+  return figure;
+}
+
+function buildNodeEl(id, kind, subtitle) {
+  const meta = KIND_BY_NAME[kind];
+
+  const li = document.createElement("li");
+  li.className = `node-card node-card--${meta.cls}`;
+  li.dataset.nodeId = id;
+  li.dataset.nodeKind = kind;
+
+  const handle = document.createElement("button");
+  handle.type = "button";
+  handle.className = "node-card__handle";
+  handle.setAttribute(
+    "aria-label",
+    `Move ${meta.label} (${id}) within its own column. Drag, or use the arrow keys.`
+  );
+  const title = document.createElement("span");
+  title.className = "node-card__title";
+  title.textContent = meta.label;
+  handle.appendChild(title);
+  li.appendChild(handle);
+
+  const sub = document.createElement("p");
+  sub.className = "node-card__subtitle";
+  sub.textContent = subtitle || "not configured yet";
+  li.appendChild(sub);
+
+  const ports = document.createElement("div");
+  ports.className = "node-card__ports";
+
+  if (meta.hasIn) {
+    const inPort = document.createElement("button");
+    inPort.type = "button";
+    inPort.className = "port port--in";
+    inPort.dataset.role = "in";
+    inPort.setAttribute("aria-label", `Connect in to ${meta.label} (${id})`);
+    inPort.addEventListener("click", () => handleInActivate(id));
+    ports.appendChild(inPort);
+  } else {
+    const spacer = document.createElement("span");
+    spacer.className = "port port--absent";
+    spacer.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
+    ports.appendChild(spacer);
+  }
+
+  if (meta.hasOut) {
+    const outPort = document.createElement("button");
+    outPort.type = "button";
+    outPort.className = "port port--out";
+    outPort.dataset.role = "out";
+    outPort.setAttribute("aria-label", `Connect out from ${meta.label} (${id})`);
+    outPort.setAttribute("aria-pressed", "false");
+    outPort.addEventListener("pointerdown", (ev) => startConnectDrag(id, outPort, ev));
